WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.221
A player moves through a game.

2
00:00:02.221 --> 00:00:03.933
A car follows a road.

3
00:00:03.933 --> 00:00:06.135
A robot reaches for an object.

4
00:00:06.135 --> 00:00:09.377
Can these different worlds share one action interface?

5
00:00:09.377 --> 00:00:13.153
Meet OneLA: Learning Many Worlds from Latent Actions.

6
00:00:14.283 --> 00:00:19.319
Control interfaces differ across domains, and action labels are often scarce.

7
00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:22.223
Video offers abundant examples of change.

8
00:00:22.223 --> 00:00:28.784
The challenge is to capture transition structure across differences in appearance, embodiment, and dynamics.

9
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:39.751
OneLA learns from one point seven nine million video clips, covering games, navigation, driving, human interactions, and robot manipulation.

10
00:00:39.751 --> 00:00:45.523
During latent action pretraining, every source shares one objective, without action labels.

11
00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:52.615
Given two frames, a latent action model infers a thirty two dimensional transition vector.

12
00:00:52.615 --> 00:01:00.327
A decoder uses this vector and the current frame to reconstruct the next, connecting the representation to observed change.

13
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:06.721
A learned orthogonal dictionary turns these coefficients into a transition embedding.

14
00:01:06.721 --> 00:01:10.775
Its geometry keeps directions distinct and fixes their scale.

15
00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:13.211
It does not assign semantic labels.

16
00:01:13.211 --> 00:01:17.374
Prediction, information limits, and sparsity guide learning together.

17
00:01:18.483 --> 00:01:21.451
The pretrained latent action model is frozen.

18
00:01:21.451 --> 00:01:27.233
Lightweight adapters map native commands into the same space using labeled trajectories.

19
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:32.845
Two control routes emerge: actions inferred from video, and actions mapped from commands.

20
00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:39.424
The world model combines latent actions with the initial frame to generate future video.

21
00:01:39.424 --> 00:01:47.158
Training jointly predicts video and realized transition actions, using the frozen latent action model for action targets.

22
00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:53.747
Here are selected generated rollouts across navigation, driving, and manipulation.

23
00:01:53.747 --> 00:01:57.139
They illustrate control through the shared latent interface.

24
00:01:57.139 --> 00:02:04.767
In quantitative benchmarks, the same trained video backbone and latent action space are used across control interfaces.

25
00:02:05.883 --> 00:02:09.981
Two more driving rollouts show how the road scene evolves over time.

26
00:02:09.981 --> 00:02:13.299
Watch the changing perspective and surrounding traffic.

27
00:02:17.883 --> 00:02:20.851
Here are two more robot manipulation rollouts.

28
00:02:20.851 --> 00:02:24.586
Watch the arm and gripper move as the interaction unfolds.

29
00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:32.746
Video can also provide the action sequence.

30
00:02:32.746 --> 00:02:36.173
These examples transfer source motion into a new scene.

31
00:02:36.173 --> 00:02:44.206
Across forty transfer cycles, OneLA reduces rotation error by twenty four point eight percent relative to OLaF.

32
00:02:45.317 --> 00:02:49.670
Frozen representations improve action readout across domains.

33
00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:57.755
Video prediction achieves the lowest motion errors on MIND and the highest PSNR and SSIM on nuScenes.

34
00:02:57.755 --> 00:03:00.041
On RT-1, it ranks second.

35
00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:10.207
In a controlled training comparison, latent actions for unlabeled videos raise the real scene preference score by twenty point five percent.

36
00:03:10.207 --> 00:03:17.173
Separately, the action prediction loss reduces human verified collisions from thirty six to twenty two percent.

37
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:30.497
OneLA brings diverse videos and native commands into a shared latent action interface, suggesting a path toward world models that learn across domains and support flexible control.
